# React.js Debugging Guidelines

## Overview
This document provides standardized debugging practices for React.js applications. Follow these guidelines to maintain consistent debugging approaches across the codebase.

## Debug Tools and Extensions

### Required Browser Extensions
- React Developer Tools
- Redux DevTools (if using Redux)
- Chrome DevTools

### VS Code Extensions
- Debugger for Chrome
- Error Lens
- ESLint

## Debugging Practices

### 1. Console Logging Best Practices
```javascript
// ❌ Avoid
console.log(data);

// ✅ Use descriptive logging
console.log('[ComponentName]:', { 
  action: 'updateState',
  previousValue: prevState,
  newValue: newState 
});
```

### 2. React DevTools Usage
- Always check component props and state
- Monitor component re-renders
- Inspect component hierarchy
- Profile performance issues

### 3. Error Boundaries
```javascript
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { hasError: false, error: null };
  
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { hasError: true, error };
  }
  
  componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {
    console.error('[ErrorBoundary]:', {
      error,
      errorInfo,
      component: this.props.componentName
    });
  }
}
```

### 4. Common Debug Points
- State updates not reflecting in UI
- Infinite re-render loops
- Memory leaks in useEffect
- Event handler issues
- API integration problems

### 5. Performance Debugging
```javascript
// Use React Profiler
import { Profiler } from 'react';

<Profiler id="ComponentName" onRender={(id, phase, actualDuration) => {
  console.log(`[${id}] ${phase}: ${actualDuration}ms`);
}}>
  <Component />
</Profiler>
```

### 6. Network Debugging
- Use Network tab in DevTools
- Implement API error logging
- Monitor request/response cycles
- Check for CORS issues

### 7. State Management Debugging
```javascript
// Redux debugging
const store = createStore(
  reducer,
  window.__REDUX_DEVTOOLS_EXTENSION__ && window.__REDUX_DEVTOOLS_EXTENSION__()
);

// Context API debugging
const MyContext = React.createContext();
const MyProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [state, setState] = useState(initialState);
  console.log('[ContextProvider] State updated:', state);
  return (
    <MyContext.Provider value={{ state, setState }}>
      {children}
    </MyContext.Provider>
  );
};
```

## Debug Commands

### Launch Debugger
```bash
# Start app in debug mode
npm start -- --inspect

# Attach VS Code debugger
{
  "type": "chrome",
  "request": "launch",
  "name": "Launch Chrome against localhost",
  "url": "http://localhost:3000",
  "webRoot": "${workspaceFolder}"
}
```

### Testing Debug
```javascript
// Jest debugging
test.only('debug this test', () => {
  // Your test code
});

// React Testing Library debug
import { screen } from '@testing-library/react';
screen.debug();
```

## Common Issues and Solutions

### 1. Stale Closures
```javascript
// ❌ Problem
useEffect(() => {
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(interval);
}, []);

// ✅ Solution
useEffect(() => {
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    setCount(prev => prev + 1);
  }, 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(interval);
}, []);
```

### 2. Memory Leaks
```javascript
// ❌ Problem
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = api.subscribe(data => {
    setState(data);
  });
}, []);

// ✅ Solution
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = api.subscribe(data => {
    setState(data);
  });
  return () => subscription.unsubscribe();
}, []);
```

## Debugging Checklist

1. [ ] Console is clear of errors and warnings
2. [ ] Components are rendering as expected
3. [ ] State updates are working correctly
4. [ ] Network requests are successful
5. [ ] Performance metrics are within acceptable range
6. [ ] Memory usage is stable
7. [ ] Error boundaries are in place
8. [ ] Event handlers are properly bound
9. [ ] Dependencies array in hooks are correct
10. [ ] Clean-up functions are implemented where needed

